course: everyday
lesson: 240
title: The night sky and outdoor myths
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A shooting star is not a star at all. It is a small piece of space rock, often no bigger than a grain of sand, that rushes into the air so fast that it heats up and burns, leaving a bright streak.
explain: The northern lights glow in the sky near the North Pole, seen from Canada, Alaska, Scotland and other far-north places; the southern lights glow in the far south, seen from New Zealand and Tasmania. Particles from the Sun make the air glow.
explain: Many things people «know» about the outdoors are myths. A good thinker asks: what is the evidence, and what is the real reason?
explain: Some myths have a grain of truth: it is fine to swim after a snack, but not when you are very full or tired. Honeybees usually die after stinging a person, but wasps and bumblebees do not.
explain: Example: a boy in Alaska tells his friend not to rub snow on her frozen cheeks. Rubbing damages frostbitten skin; she should warm it slowly instead.
fact: A shooting star is a small piece of space rock burning up high in the air, not a star.
fact: A shooting star glows because it heats up from rushing through the air.
fact: Go out to look at the stars with a grown-up, because in the dark it is hard to see dangers.
fact: The northern lights may be seen in Canada, Alaska, Scotland and other far-north places, and the southern lights in New Zealand and Tasmania.
fact: Particles from the Sun are pulled towards the poles and make the air glow there, and that glow is the northern and southern lights.
fact: Swimming after a snack is fine; the old one-hour rule has no real evidence, but do not swim when very full or tired.
fact: A honeybee's sting is barbed and gets stuck in skin, so a honeybee usually dies after stinging a person.
fact: Wasps and bumblebees can sting more than once and do not die.
fact: You cannot tell a safe mushroom by how it looks or smells; deadly mushrooms can look, smell and taste nice, so never eat wild ones.
fact: Rubbing snow on frozen skin harms it; warm frostbitten skin slowly instead.
fact: Urine does not help a jellyfish sting and can make some worse; follow local first aid.
fact: Touching a toad does not give you warts, because warts are caused by a human virus, but wash your hands after touching any animal.
exercise: What is a shooting star? | A small piece of space rock burning up in the air | A real star falling down out of the sky towards us | A planet moving quickly across the sky | A satellite blinking its lights on and off as it passes over
exercise: Why does a shooting star glow? | It heats up rushing through the air | It reflects light from the Moon | It is on fire before it reaches Earth | It carries sunlight from far away
exercise: Where might a child see the northern lights? | In Canada, Alaska or Scotland | In Florida or southern Texas | In Australia's tropical north | In New Zealand's North Island
exercise: Where can the southern lights be seen? | From places far south, such as New Zealand and Tasmania | Only from the North Pole, and nowhere else on Earth | From anywhere on Earth on a clear, dark night with no Moon | From hot deserts near the equator
exercise: What makes the northern lights? | Particles from the Sun make the air glow | Moonlight reflecting off the ice | Light from big cities bouncing off clouds | Ice crystals catching the light of stars
exercise: You ate a sandwich ten minutes ago. Can you go swimming? | Yes, a snack is fine, but not when very full or tired | No, you must always wait exactly one hour after eating anything | No, you will get a cramp and sink at once | Yes, but only if you swim very fast to burn it off
exercise: Does a bee always die when it stings? | A honeybee usually does, but wasps and bumblebees do not | Yes, every single bee and every wasp dies after it stings | No, bees never die from stinging | Only bumblebees die after stinging, and honeybees never do
exercise: Why does a honeybee usually die after stinging a person? | Its barbed sting gets stuck in the skin | It uses up all of its poison in one go | It is far too frightened to fly ever again | People always swat it straight away
exercise: A mushroom smells nice and looks like one from the shop. Is it safe to eat? | No, only an expert can tell, and you should never eat wild ones | Yes, a nice smell always means that a mushroom is safe | Yes, if it looks just like a shop mushroom | Yes, if you have seen an animal nibble it first and stay well
exercise: A friend's fingers are white and frozen after sledging. Should she rub them with snow? | No, rubbing harms frozen skin; warm them slowly | Yes, rubbing with snow helps the blood come back | Yes, rubbing hard is the fastest cure there is | Only if the snow is fresh and clean
exercise: Someone is stung by a jellyfish. Does peeing on it help? | No, it does not help; follow local first aid | Yes, it is the best cure there is for all kinds of stings | Yes, but only for stings from the big jellyfish | Only if it is done in the very first minute after the sting
exercise: Can touching a toad give you warts? | No, warts come from a human virus | Yes, toad skin spreads warts to anyone who holds it | Only if the toad is green and very bumpy | Only if you touch it at night, when its skin is wet
exercise: What should you still do after holding a toad? | Wash your hands | Rub your eyes to cool them | Nothing, toads are perfectly clean | Kiss it so it is not scared
test: A girl in Tasmania sees green and pink light rippling in the southern sky at night. What is she seeing? | The southern lights | A shooting star | Lights from a ship far out at sea | A rainbow at night
test: Your little brother makes a wish on a shooting star and says it is a star falling. What can you tell him? | It is a tiny space rock burning up in the air | It is a real star that has fallen all the way down to Earth | It is a plane flying very fast and very high up | It is the Moon throwing out sparks as it turns round
test: Which statement is a myth? | You must wait a full hour after any snack before swimming | A honeybee usually dies after stinging a person | Rubbing frozen skin can damage it | Deadly mushrooms can look nice
test: A wasp stings you once. What is true? | It can sting again, because wasps do not die after stinging | It will drop dead straight away, just like every honeybee does | Its sting stays stuck in your skin | It cannot sting a second person
test: A friend says, «This mushroom is safe, it smells lovely.» What is the best reply? | Deadly mushrooms can smell and taste nice, so we never eat wild ones | Let us taste a small bit first to check it | If it smells lovely, it must be safe to eat | Let us cook it for a long time, because cooking will make it safe
test: A boy in Alberta has numb, white cheeks after a cold walk. What should his family do? | Warm his cheeks slowly indoors | Rub his cheeks hard with clean snow | Put his face near a hot fire at once | Send him back outside to get used to it
test: On a beach in Queensland, a boy is stung by a jellyfish. Someone shouts «Pee on it!» What is better? | Follow local first aid and get a lifeguard | Pee on it, because that always works on stings | Rub wet sand into the sting hard to get it out | Ignore it, because all jellyfish stings are harmless
test: Why do the northern lights appear near the poles and not over Florida? | The Sun's particles are pulled towards the poles, where they make the air glow | The poles are much colder, and very cold air always glows on its own in the dark | Ice at the poles shines light up into the sky | Florida is far too close to the Sun for the lights to be able to form over it at night
test: saw a shooting star!! is that a star falling | No, it's a bit of space rock burning up | Yes, a whole star just fell out of the sky | Yes, it's a star dying n falling to bits | It's an aeroplane light flashing as it flies past
test: just ate a sandwich, mum says wait an hour b4 swimming. true? | No, a snack is fine, just not when very full or tired | Yes, or you'll get a cramp n sink straight away | Yes, exactly sixty minutes | Wait two hours to be totally safe, an hour isn't enough
test: touched a toad, will i get warts now | No, warts come from a human virus, just wash your hands | Yes, toads cause warts | Only if it was a big toad with lots of bumps on it | Yes, on the hand that touched it, in about a week
test: jellyfish sting, my mate says wee on it | No, wee doesn't help, use local first aid | Yes, wee stops the sting straight away | Yes, and rub sand on it | Only if it really really hurts, then it works
explain: A boy in Arizona looked straight at the Sun during an eclipse; it burned a spot in his eye and his eyesight was damaged for life.
explain: A girl in Scotland believed that moss always grows on the north side of trees and used it to find her way; she walked the wrong way and got lost.
fact: Looking at the Sun, even during an eclipse (when the Moon passes in front of the Sun), can damage your eyes for good; sunglasses are not enough, only proper eclipse glasses are safe.
fact: Old sayings like "moss grows on the north side" are not reliable ways to find your way, because moss grows wherever it is damp and shady, on any side of a tree.
test: eclipse today, gonna look with sunglasses | No, only use proper eclipse glasses | Yes, dark sunglasses work fine | Yes, if you blink a lot | Only for a second or two, that's safe
test: Why is moss a bad guide for finding north? | It grows wherever it is damp and shady, on any side | It only ever grows on the south side of trees | It grows only on dead trees | It is only wrong in the summer, when it grows faster
test: wanna walk home alone in the dark to see the stars | No, go with an adult, it's hard to see dangers | Yes, the stars are bright enough to see by | Yes, with a torch | Only if it's warm out n not raining
test: did u hear a kid stared at the eclipse with no glasses and now he can't see properly? real lesson? | Never look at the Sun, even in an eclipse | Eclipses are safe to look at | Look only for a few seconds | Sunglasses make it safe
test: apparently my cousin's friend got stung by a jellyfish and they weed on it and it got better straight away. true lesson? | Wee doesn't help and can make it worse; follow local first aid | Wee always cures a sting | Wee works, but only if it's fresh and you do it in the first minute | Rub sand on it hard instead, that's how you get all the stingers out
test: my mate swears he got warts from picking up a toad. probably just a myth? | Yes, warts come from a human virus, not toads | No, toads always give warts | No, but only frogs give warts | No, only if you kiss it
exam: Identify the nature of a shooting star. | A small piece of space rock burning up high in the air | A star falling towards the Earth | A satellite exploding in orbit high above the atmosphere | A spark thrown off from the surface of the Moon
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the honeybee upon stinging a person? | Its barbed sting lodges in the skin and it usually dies | It may sting repeatedly without any harm to itself | It loses no part of itself | It becomes more aggressive and proceeds to sting again
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning frostbitten skin. | It is warmed slowly and is never rubbed with snow | It is rubbed vigorously with snow | It is placed at once in very hot water | It is left exposed to the cold until the feeling returns
exam: Ascertain the cause of the northern and southern lights. | Particles from the Sun cause the air near the poles to glow | Moonlight reflected from polar ice | Lightning storms high above the clouds | Fires burning in the far north
